{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"STATUS FOR HANDOFF — the messaging fix LANDED (change 1027, MERGED); the ticket is NOT fully done and should stay open.\n\nDONE (shipped in 1027):\n * A bounded-recovery failure no longer inherits the false 'install the agents extra / set a model API key' remedy. It now reports the measured size against the limit and points at the gate_error_v1 sidecar.\n * The size claim is conditional on the bound keys being present, so a recovery failure from another stage (e.g. exhaustion at criterion 7) does not get a fabricated 'context (None chars) exceeds the limit (None chars)'.\n * A genuine unavailability STILL gets the install-the-extra guidance — pinned by a negative control so narrowing this cannot decay into deleting it.\n * returncode stays 1, not 11: the bounds re-apply on every attempt, so advertising it as retryable would loop an auto-retrying agent.\n * Tests: tests/unit/test_close_gate_bound_message_d59e.py (4), mutation-verified.\n\nNOT DONE — deliberately, and this is the remaining scope:\n * AC 'a ticket whose description alone exceeds _MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS can still complete a close without --force-close' is met only via its OR branch (the failure now explains precisely what to shorten). An oversized ticket still cannot USE bounded recovery.\n * AC 'the interaction between _MAX_TOTAL_CRITERIA_CHARS (32,000) and _MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS (24,000) is documented or reconciled' is untouched.\nWHY NOT: /rebar-debug classified the bound itself as INTENDED BEHAVIOUR, not a defect — it is a documented fail-closed cost safeguard (docs/llm-framework.md:487-508) pinned by a contract test asserting ZERO billable recovery calls on a breach. Making an oversized ticket recoverable means adding an escalation ladder (the plan-review path's sanctioned shape: batch criteria -> one-at-a-time -> larger model window -> a typed 'reduce the ticket' finding; see plan_review/orchestrator.py:6-15 and det_floor.py:619-656). That is a DESIGN CHANGE, not a bug fix, so it was left for a human decision rather than smuggled in under a bug ticket.\nPRACTICAL NOTE for whoever picks this up: the failure is entered only when the PRIMARY verifier truncates, which is a property of the live call — so retrying a blocked close often succeeds. That is how c97f was eventually closed. It is a workaround, not a fix."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785434696072131001,"uuid":"9c42d669-4745-452f-9e05-b389d968faed"}